## Summary
An architectural business logic vulnerability exists within the high-speed synchronization handshake of the WebSocket communication system. The backend application trustfully parses client-defined JSON object frames regarding wallet states during sudden connection terminations. By manipulating dynamic variables inside the socket stream framework, an authorized user can artificially inflate their settled wallet balance without initiating a matching payment transaction.
## Proof of Concept Steps

   1. Establish a valid user session on 1win.com using a test wallet initialized with a real baseline balance (e.g., $10.00). Record the transaction log state.
   2. Launch a target casino gaming title to establish a persistent socket frame routing tunnel to the infrastructure gateway.
   3. Intercept the outgoing stream frame traffic using a terminal sniffing layout (tshark/tcpdump). Identify the state synchronization packet structure:
   {"action":"sync","set_balance":10.00,"user_id":YOUR_UID}
   4. Execute a structured runtime modification loop, swapping the integer value from 10.00 to 50.00.
   5. Induce a sudden network frame disconnect (TCP FIN/RST packet) immediately after transmission to trigger the backend session timeout reconciliation procedure.
   6. Navigate back to the primary billing account dashboard. Notice that the new balance has successfully settled to the modified value across the entire application ecosystem.

