## 📡 Part 2: Real-Time Automated WebSocket Stream Monitor
To watch variables shift dynamically as you execute tests, click through different game lobbies, or switch currency settings, you can deploy a live-tailing script interface. This lightweight Python automation script taps into your captured terminal network logs and instantly alerts you whenever critical wallet or user identifiers change in the stream.
## The Live Monitor Code (stream_monitor.py)
Save this file onto your computer:

nano stream_monitor.py

Paste the following script:

import sysimport reimport jsonimport time
# Define the local log target created by your tshark extraction commandLOG_FILE = "extracted_frames.txt"
# Regex rules targeting high-value financial parametersBALANCE_REGEX = re.compile(r'"(balance|amount|win_amount|payout)"\s*:\s*([0-9.]+)')USER_ID_REGEX = re.compile(r'"(uid|user_id|player_id)"\s*:\s*"([^"]+)"')

print("[*] Launching Live WebSocket Stream Monitor...")
print("[*] Tracking modifications across balance variables and target IDs. Press Ctrl+C to terminate.")
print("-" * 75)
def monitor_stream():
    try:
        with open(LOG_FILE, "r", errors="ignore") as f:
            # Move read position to the end of the current file data stream
            f.seek(0, 2)
            
            while True:
                line = f.readline()
                if not line:
                    time.sleep(0.1)  # Sleep briefly to wait for incoming frame data chunks
                    continue
                
                # Check line for target matches
                balance_match = BALANCE_REGEX.search(line)
                uid_match = USER_ID_REGEX.search(line)
                
                if balance_match or uid_match:
                    print(f"\n[ALERT] Active Transaction Stream Transition Detected:")
                    print(f" -> Raw Payload: {line.strip()[:120]}...") # Print a short sample of the raw frame
                    
                    if uid_match:
                        print(f" -> Isolated Context [Target ID]: {uid_match.group(2)}")
                    if balance_match:
                        print(f" -> Isolated Context [Financial State]: {balance_match.group(2)}")
                    print("-" * 40)
                    
    except KeyboardInterrupt:
        print("\n[-] Stream monitor session ended cleanly.")
    except FileNotFoundError:
        print(f"[-] Operational Error: Target file '{LOG_FILE}' not found. Verify your capture pipeline.")
if __name__ == "__main__":
    monitor_stream()

## How to Execute It Live:

   1. Start your tshark extraction stream script to continuously output web socket data directly into extracted_frames.txt:
   
   tshark -l -i wlan0 -Y "websocket.payload.text" -T fields -e websocket.payload.text >> extracted_frames.txt &
   
   2. In a second terminal window, run the monitor tool:
   
   python3 stream_monitor.py
   
   
Every time a transaction completes, a game registers a state change, or a balance update triggers, your screen will display the clean isolated values. Look for deviations where variables change unexpectedly or keys leak across different game environments.
